\section*{Bounded approximation in uniformly discrete Lipschitz-free spaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 330; 2026-09-26), the asserted result is that a countable uniformly discrete metric space has a real Lipschitz-free space with the approximation property but without any bounded approximation constant.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 330; 2026-09-26), colored-graph blocks carry signed measures and opposing local bump tests that force large finite-rank norms. Each block still admits coefficient truncation, but a metric wedge joins blocks with unbounded required constants, preserving AP and defeating BAP.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 330; 2026-09-26).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 330; 2026-09-26), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: a countable uniformly discrete metric space has a real Lipschitz-free space with the approximation property but without any bounded approximation constant.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DiscreteLipschitzFree.lean}, \texttt{RealL1Renorming.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DiscreteFree.real\_l1\_quantitative\_renorming}, \texttt{OAI.DiscreteFree.source\_endpoints}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

